    "dc_steps": {
      "type": "c-content-panel",
      "blocks": {
        "dc_steps_heading_0": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How to Wash at Home"
          }
        },
        "dc_steps_text_1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>01.</strong> Empty all pockets.</p><p><strong>02.</strong> Turn the product inside out.</p><p><strong>03.</strong> Roll or fold it and place it in a mesh laundry bag. For sleeping bags, the included mesh storage bag works too.</p><p><strong>04.</strong> We recommend a down-specific detergent. A mild, neutral household detergent also works.</p><p><strong>05.</strong> Wash on your machine's hand-wash, delicate, or bulky/blanket cycle.</p><p><em>Regular cycles spin faster and can damage the fabric. Washing with a few large towels helps protect it. Wetting the product first keeps it from floating so it washes evenly. Only do this while the machine is stopped, to avoid injury or damage to the machine.</em></p><p><strong>06.</strong> Spin thoroughly on the cycle's spin setting. If it's still wet, gently squeeze out the excess water by hand.</p><p><strong>07.</strong> Press out excess moisture with large towels.</p>"
          }
        },
        "dc_steps_text_2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>08.</strong> Dry completely in a well-ventilated spot out of direct sunlight.</p><p><em>Avoid washing during humid weather, which can cause odors and mildew. Even when the surface feels dry, the down inside may still be damp, which can also lead to mildew and odors. If you can't dry it in the shade, cover the product with a sheet to keep it out of direct sun. Sleeping bags dry best draped in a U shape.</em></p><p><strong>09.</strong> Once dry, pat the whole product evenly with both hands to break up the down. If you can, repeat once a day.</p><p><em>Sleeping bag baffles connect front to back, so work the down back and forth between the two sides.</em></p><p><strong>10.</strong> If down is poking through the quilting, clean it up with a lint roller.</p><p><strong>11.</strong> Finally, spray the quilted seams with a waterproofing spray.</p><p><em>It won't stop down from escaping entirely, but it helps seal the stitch holes.</em></p>"
          }
        }
      },
